f(17,'f_t et f_s','last paragraph570','Main and witness581 both print f, not g. With h:Y→S, the exact relations are f=h∘g, f_t=h_t∘g_t and f_s=h_s∘g_s. The source assertion that f_t and f_s are lci is true under the printed hypotheses: each regular finite-type fiber locally embeds as a closed subscheme of a regular affine space over its field, followed by the smooth projection. The kernel of the local surjection from a regular local ambient ring to the regular local fiber ring is generated by a regular sequence, giving the factorization of VIII1.1. The same construction over the regular Y_t and Y_s proves that g_t and g_s are lci. In the proper-pushforward case stated at the start of Remark7.10.3, quasi-projectivity makes these proper maps projective, so VIII3.2 applies to them and yields the graded maps in the square analogous to7.10.1bis. Direct inspection of VIII1.1 on466 and VIII3.2 on483 confirms the exact factorization and filtered-pushforward dependencies. The source never identifies f_* with g_*: indeed for g=id of P¹_S, g_{t*} is the identity on K(P¹), whereas f_{t*} is the Euler-characteristic map to Z. Thus the printed assertion and its stated consequence both have verified proofs; replacing the true f_t,f_s clause solely to make the implicit route explicit would be an unnecessary emendation.','reverted inferred-slip verdict','Retain f_t,f_s in both layers; no source misprint. Record the compositions and proof in the apparatus only.',batch='566_570');D[-1]['misprint']=False
