f(10,'X_s×_{k(x)}C_s','lower-right vertex574','Both scans have k(x). The displayed square is the base change of X×_S C along s→S, with the canonical isomorphism (X×_S C)×_S s ≅ X_s×_{k(s)}C_s induced by the two projections and the fibre-product universal property. The x in the preceding definition is a section S→C. If its closed image is denoted x_s, then the section gives k(s)→k(x_s)→k(s) with composite identity, hence k(x_s)=k(s) through these maps. The current k(s) explicitly names the base field of the stated fibre product, without changing that identified product; retain the source k(x) diplomatically.',batch='571_575')
